# 4. Four-module serial mode

## Required board modification

Set each module to serial/SPI mode by moving its interface-selection resistor or solder jumper:

```text
PAR / 8080  →  SER / SPI
```

Exact resistor names depend on the PCB revision.

Required AD7606 mode state:

```text
PAR/SER/BYTE SEL = HIGH (move resistor)
DB15/BYTE SEL    = LOW
```

Ground unused parallel data pins unless the module already grounds them internally:

```text
DB0–DB6  → GND
DB9–DB15 → GND
```

Do not ground:

```text
DB7 → DOUTA
DB8 → DOUTB
```

## Shared serial wiring

| AD7606 pin on all modules | GIGA connection |
|---|---|
| `RD/SCLK` | GIGA SPI `SCK`, normally `D13` |
| `DB7/DOUTA` | GIGA SPI `CIPO/MISO`, normally `D12` |
| Tied `CVA + CVB` | Example `D39` |
| `RST` | Example `D40` |
| `+5V` | External regulated 5 V |
| `VIO` | GIGA `3.3V` |
| `GND` | Common ground |

Connect all four `DB7/DOUTA` pins to the same GIGA CIPO/MISO line.

Only one module may have `CS` low at a time.

The original AD7606 does not require MOSI/COPI for normal hardware-mode data reading.

## Separate serial signals

| Function | Module 1 | Module 2 | Module 3 | Module 4 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| `CS` | `D41` | `D42` | `D43` | `D44` |
| `BUSY` | `D45` | `D46` | `D47` | `D48` |

## Optional dual-output serial mode

| Module pin | Serial function |
|---|---|
| `DB7` | `DOUTA` |
| `DB8` | `DOUTB` |

Use only `DOUTA` for the initial implementation.

- One data output: 128 serial clock pulses per module
- Two data outputs: 64 serial clock pulses per module

## Serial acquisition sequence

```text
1. Set CS1–CS4 HIGH.
2. Pulse the shared CVA/CVB line HIGH, then LOW.
3. Wait until BUSY1–BUSY4 are all LOW.
4. Set CS1 LOW.
5. Read eight signed 16-bit values from DOUTA.
6. Set CS1 HIGH.
7. Repeat for CS2, CS3 and CS4.
```

```cpp
pulseConvst();

while (
    digitalRead(BUSY1) ||
    digitalRead(BUSY2) ||
    digitalRead(BUSY3) ||
    digitalRead(BUSY4)
) {
    // Apply a timeout.
}

SPI.beginTransaction(
    SPISettings(5000000, MSBFIRST, SPI_MODE2)
);

for (uint8_t board = 0; board < 4; board++) {
    digitalWrite(csPins[board], LOW);

    for (uint8_t channel = 0; channel < 8; channel++) {
        const uint16_t highByte = SPI.transfer(0x00);
        const uint16_t lowByte  = SPI.transfer(0x00);

        adcValues[board * 8 + channel] =
            static_cast<int16_t>((highByte << 8) | lowByte);
    }

    digitalWrite(csPins[board], HIGH);
}

SPI.endTransaction();
```

Verify the SPI mode and clock frequency during hardware bring-up.

